course: chinese-street
lesson: 27
title: Lanterns, dragon boats, mooncakes and birthdays
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: The Chinese year is full of festivals, all set by the moon: fifteen days after New Year comes Yuánxiāo jié, the Lantern Festival, at the first full moon, and only then is New Year really over.
explain: In early summer comes Duānwǔ jié, the Dragon Boat Festival, with races to a drumbeat and sticky rice parcels, zòngzi; it remembers a poet who drowned, so some families wish "Duānwǔ ānkāng" (a peaceful Duanwu) instead of "happy".
explain: In September or October comes Zhōngqiū jié, the Mid-Autumn or Moon Festival: families cut a rich mooncake into small pieces and look at the full moon for the lady Cháng'é and her jade rabbit.
explain: Birthdays have their own food: chángshòumiàn, long-life noodles — don't bite them short, slurp them whole, because long noodles mean long life.
explain: One day in early April is not a party at all: Qīngmíng jié, Tomb-Sweeping Day, when families quietly visit and clean the graves of their grandparents and ancestors.
fact: "Yuánxiāo jié" is the Lantern Festival, fifteen days after New Year, at the first full moon of the year; it ends the New Year season.
fact: "tāngyuán" are sweet rice balls in warm syrup, often with black sesame inside; their round shape means the family together, and they are hot inside.
fact: "dēnglong" means lantern; "cāi dēngmí", guessing riddles written on lanterns, is a Lantern Festival game.
fact: "Duānwǔ jié" is the Dragon Boat Festival, in early summer on the 5th day of the 5th lunar month; it remembers a poet who drowned, and some families wish "Duānwǔ ānkāng".
fact: "zòngzi" are sticky rice parcels wrapped in bamboo leaves and tied with string; you don't eat the leaves, and sweet versus salty filling starts north–south family arguments.
fact: "sài lóngzhōu" means dragon boat racing; the drummer at the front sets the beat, and there are races in British cities too.
fact: "Zhōngqiū jié" is the Mid-Autumn or Moon Festival, in September or October at the full moon; it is a family reunion night second only to New Year.
fact: "yuèbing" means mooncake, very rich, shared and cut into small pieces.
fact: "shǎng yuè" means looking at the full moon together; the story says the lady Cháng'é lives on the moon with a jade rabbit.
fact: "shēngrì kuàilè" means happy birthday; "zhù nǐ shēngrì kuàilè" is sung to the same tune as the English song.
fact: "chángshòumiàn" are longevity noodles eaten on birthdays, especially grandparents'; you don't cut or bite them short, because long noodles mean long life.
fact: "Qīngmíng jié" is Tomb-Sweeping Day in early April, a quiet, respectful day of visiting family graves; you don't say "happy Qingming".
exercise: When is Yuánxiāo jié? | Fifteen days after New Year, at the full moon | On New Year's Eve, just before the clock strikes midnight | In early summer, with the boat races | In early April
exercise: Lín's granny serves round sweet rice balls in warm syrup. What are they? | tāngyuán | zòngzi | yuèbing | jiǎozi
exercise: Why should you bite tāngyuán carefully? | They're very hot inside | They have a lucky coin inside | They're made of crushed ice | They have little fish bones inside
exercise: Kids carry red paper "dēnglong" at the festival. What are they? | Lanterns | Kites | Drums | Fans
exercise: What is "cāi dēngmí"? | Guessing riddles written on lanterns | Lighting fireworks in the street at midnight | Racing boats on the river | Folding dumplings
exercise: When is Duānwǔ jié? | Early summer, the 5th day of the 5th lunar month | Late January, at the very start of the lunar year | September or October, at the full moon | Early April
exercise: Why do some families say "Duānwǔ ānkāng" instead of "kuàilè"? | It remembers a poet who drowned | "kuàilè" is a rude word for that day | "ānkāng" means "big party" | It's only said to grandparents
exercise: Lín unwraps a pyramid of sticky rice from bamboo leaves. What is it? | zòngzi | tāngyuán | mántou | yuèbing
exercise: Who sets the beat in sài lóngzhōu? | The drummer at the front | The fastest paddler in the middle | The captain at the back | The crowd on the bank
exercise: What is Zhōngqiū jié? | The Mid-Autumn or Moon Festival | The Lantern Festival | The Dragon Boat Festival | Tomb-Sweeping Day
exercise: Lín's mum cuts one yuèbing into eight. Why? | Mooncakes are very rich and are shared | There's only one knife in the house, a big one | Mooncakes are poisonous whole | Eight is the number of the moon
exercise: The family goes into the garden to "shǎng yuè". What do they do? | Look at the full moon together | Race little paper boats on the pond | Light firecrackers in the street | Sweep the graves of the whole family
exercise: On the moon, who lives with Cháng'é? | A jade rabbit | A golden dragon | A white tiger cub | A shining silver fish
exercise: It's Lín's birthday. What do you say? | shēngrì kuàilè! | xīnnián kuàilè! | gōngxǐ fācái! | Duānwǔ ānkāng!
exercise: At Grandad's birthday you get a bowl of chángshòumiàn. How do you eat it? | Slurp the noodles whole, don't bite them | Cut them into short bits so you look neat and polite | Leave them all for Grandad to eat | Eat only the soup
exercise: What do families do on Qīngmíng jié? | Visit and clean the graves of ancestors | Watch the full moon in the garden | Race dragon boats | Give red envelopes to all the little children
exercise: How can you sing "Happy Birthday" in Mandarin? | "zhù nǐ shēngrì kuàilè", same tune | "xīnnián kuàilè", a new tune | "gōngxǐ fācái", same tune | "wànshì rúyì", a new tune
test: my mate wished Lín's family "happy Qingming!!" with a big grin. good idea? | No, it's a quiet day of remembering the dead | Yes, it's a big party with fireworks | Yes, it's a birthday | No, you should say gōngxǐ fācái
test: You cut Grandad's birthday noodles into short bits to eat them neatly. What might the family think? | Long noodles mean long life — don't cut them | Cutting them brings double luck to Grandad this year | Short noodles are more polite | Nothing, noodles don't matter at all
test: In a cartoon a girl eats a zòngzi leaves and all. What's wrong? | You don't eat the bamboo leaves | Nothing, the leaves are the best bit | Zòngzi have no leaves | You eat only the leaves
test: Lín's granny from the north and auntie from the south argue about zòngzi. What's it about? | Sweet versus salty filling | Whether anyone should eat them at all | The colour of the string around them | Which day of the year to eat them
test: A friend wants to eat a whole mooncake alone after dinner. What would probably happen? | It's far too rich — families share small slices | It's just a small biscuit, so that's fine for one kid | It's healthy, eat two | It's only decoration, not food at all
test: Lín says New Year ends on New Year's Day. His granny disagrees. Why? | It isn't over until the Lantern Festival | It ends on Christmas Day | It never ends | It goes on until the Moon Festival in autumn
test: Which festival is a family reunion night second only to New Year? | Zhōngqiū jié, the Moon Festival | Qīngmíng jié, Tomb-Sweeping Day | Duānwǔ jié, Dragon Boat Festival | Yuánxiāo jié, Lantern Festival
test: What would happen to a dragon boat whose paddlers ignore the drummer? | They'd lose the rhythm and slow down | They'd go much faster without the noise | They'd lift off and fly over the water | Nothing, the drum is just for decoration
test: Why are tāngyuán round, the family says? | Round means the family together | Round ones roll into the pot faster | Round looks like a shiny gold coin | Round is much easier to chew and swallow
test: Lín looks at the full moon and says he sees the rabbit. What story is he thinking of? | Cháng'é and the jade rabbit | The monster nián | The Monkey King | The drowned poet
test: Order these from the start of the year: Moon Festival, Lantern Festival, Dragon Boat Festival. | Lantern, Dragon Boat, Moon | Moon, Lantern, Dragon Boat | Dragon Boat, Moon, Lantern | Lantern, Moon, Dragon Boat
test: Lín's cousin says all Chinese festivals are parties. Which one shows he's wrong? | Qīngmíng jié, a quiet day at family graves | Yuánxiāo jié, with lanterns and riddles | Zhōngqiū jié, with mooncakes | Duānwǔ jié, with boat races
exam: Identify the festival held fifteen days after the Chinese New Year, coinciding with the first full moon of the year. | Yuánxiāo jié | Zhōngqiū jié | Duānwǔ jié | Qīngmíng jié
exam: It is given that the Dragon Boat Festival commemorates a poet who drowned. Select the greeting consistent with this association as preferred by certain families. | Duānwǔ ānkāng | Duānwǔ fācái | Duānwǔ hóngbāo | Duānwǔ wǎn'ān
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consumption of longevity noodles upon a birthday? | They are eaten unbroken, length signifying long life | The noodles are severed to signify a fresh start in the new year | The noodles are reserved for the youngest guest | The noodles are consumed cold at midnight
exam: Ascertain the character of the observance designated "Qīngmíng jié". | A solemn day for visiting and tending ancestral graves | A festival of riotous celebration with fireworks | A day for the exchange of red envelopes | A night for viewing the full moon
